  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∃\\,n\\in N,\\,∀m\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a natural $n$ such that, for every natural $m,$ we have $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $m\\le 10$ is not true for some natural $m.$ Say, for $m=11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The predicate\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,∀\\,m\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nis false for any $n\\in N.$ For example, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∀\\,m\\in N,\\,m\\le 1\n$$\n\nis false since $m=2$ is not less than or equal to $1.$  Similarly,\n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∀\\,m\\in N,\\,m\\le 2\n$$\n\nis false since $m=3$ is not less than or equal to $2.$ And so on. In general, it's sufficient to take $m=n+1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∃\\,n\\in N,\\,∀m\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∃\\,n\\in N\\,(\\,{{∀m\\in N,\\,m\\le n}_{⏟}}_{P(n)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(n)$ has a nonempty truth set. And according to statement II, this is not he case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
